WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.224
Kettle is a small platformer built for the course
Frame Perfect, named after the copper kettle you steer.

00:00:07.274 --> 00:00:14.754
A run is a file with one line of buttons for each frame,
and the game plays that file back the same way every time.

00:00:14.804 --> 00:00:21.682
On level 2 the flag sits behind a tall wall, and
the intended route climbs the platforms over it.

00:00:21.732 --> 00:00:25.865
Par is the frame count the
level sets as its target.

00:00:25.915 --> 00:00:32.153
With the game working as its makers
meant, the best run takes 186 frames.

00:00:32.203 --> 00:00:42.307
Could any run finish within par?
Decide before the next result.

00:00:42.357 --> 00:00:46.939
A breadth-first search assumes nothing
about how the game should behave.

00:00:46.989 --> 00:00:52.444
It tries every input on every frame and
merges runs that reach the same state.

00:00:52.494 --> 00:01:01.033
Its run is 116 frames shorter than the intended best,
so something moves Kettle faster than its makers meant.

00:01:01.083 --> 00:01:06.540
Here is that run near the wall, with position
and speed read from the game's memory.

00:01:06.590 --> 00:01:17.819
Left presses on frames 64 and 66 each take half a pixel off the
speed, which leaves the front edge just short of the wall on frame 66.

00:01:17.869 --> 00:01:28.115
On the next frame, does it stop,
get pushed back, or go through?

00:01:28.165 --> 00:01:29.953
It goes through.

00:01:30.003 --> 00:01:35.187
The game moves Kettle first, then tests
only the edge it is moving toward.

00:01:35.237 --> 00:01:40.257
If that edge is inside a wall, the game
pushes Kettle out on the nearer side.

00:01:40.307 --> 00:01:47.626
At walking speed the edge ends at most one and a half
pixels in, so the nearer side is the one it came from.

00:01:47.676 --> 00:01:54.677
At five pixels the edge ends past the middle of the
8-pixel tile, and the nearer side is the far one.

00:01:54.727 --> 00:02:04.019
Near the start, a belt adds half a pixel a frame to walking speed,
which makes two, and a jump made at that speed adds half a pixel more.

00:02:04.069 --> 00:02:13.429
The cap that holds Kettle to walking speed runs only on ground frames
without a jump, so a jump on the frame Kettle lands keeps every boost.

00:02:13.479 --> 00:02:17.643
Eight of those jumps take
it to six pixels a frame.

00:02:17.693 --> 00:02:25.219
In Frame Perfect you write the search that found this run,
and a minimiser that keeps only the presses the clip needs.

00:02:25.269 --> 00:02:32.415
The capstone is level 3, which you
finish with your own tools and no lesson.
